{"stats":{"took":62,"total":12,"estimate":false,"maxScore":173.62506},"results":[{"_meta":{"score":173.62506},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20190025239,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"b5edbe967b6141baab7bc01849b58d08","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Behavioral Sciences"],"keywords":["Normative Data","Fine Motor Skills","Cognition"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","eccnNumber":"","submissionId":20190025239,"ear":"NO","id":"a5b80dc2a7674226ad1fd3aaf7bff867","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["ARC-E-DAA-TN68057","Report Number: ARC-E-DAA-TN68057"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"NNX17AE07A","submissionId":20190025239,"id":"901de94f74f44e868916c8b09c995534","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NNJ15HK11B","submissionId":20190025239,"id":"9679a456c04b48a8831400ebb91a6186","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"344494.02.01.10","submissionId":20190025239,"id":"953e286a334648fb98dfbebfe32132da","type":"WBS"}],"title":"The Fine Motor Skills and Cognition Test Batteries: Normative Data and Interdependencies","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2019-05-20T15:19:10.0770000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20190025239,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Beard, Bettina L."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Ames Research Center","location":"Moffett Field, CA, United States"}},"id":"4b4202aefb7043bcb191eee2f60237be"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20190025239,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ahumada, Albert J., Jr."},"organization":{"name":"San Jose State Univ.","location":"Moffett Field, CA, United States"}},"id":"30980c26d1454062808f2c775f404859"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20190025239,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Holden, Kritina L."},"organization":{"name":"Wyle Labs., Inc.","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"dc65ce5b8ebc4373ae45f7239194843c"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"NASA_TECHNICAL_MANAGEMENT","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20190025239,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2019-05-20T15:19:10.0770000+00:00","center":{"code":"ARC","name":"Ames Research Center","id":"4540cd94d24c4bf29f8773a27faf96b2"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Fine motor skills and cognitive abilities are major contributors to crew performance on essentially all extravehicular and intra-vehicular activities during spaceflight. It is critical for the crew’s safety, and for mission productivity, to know if, and when, motor skills or cognitive abilities are compromised so that countermeasures may be introduced. NASA has developed two test batteries to measure and monitor astronaut cognitive and fine motor skills. The Cognition Test Battery contains 10 sub-tests that assess cognitive behaviors ranging from low level visual perception to high level decision-making. The Fine Motor Skills Test Battery contains 4 sub-tests that assess finger dexterity, manual dexterity and wrist-finger speed. This study sought to determine acceptable norms for both batteries in an astronaut-like population and to identify the extent to which fine motor skills contribute to cognitive test scores.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"submissionId":20190025239,"name":"International Association for the Advancement of Space Safety","location":"Noordwijk, Netherlands","id":"3248d77190d94a0ba724f37e5de4b35f"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20190025239,"endDate":"2019-05-17T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"International Association for the Advancement of Space Safety","location":"Noordwijk, Netherlands"}},"meetingId":"ed32f165d58f495bae8ef5579c16a2a6","id":"a1a8de2e5e4645d7b0836fb607d74b5a"}],"name":"Annual International Association for the Advancement of Space Safety Conference (IAASS 2019): Space Safety is no Accident","location":"El Segundo, CA","id":"ed32f165d58f495bae8ef5579c16a2a6","startDate":"2019-05-15T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20190025239,"id":"c32badf26e60443fbd1ae27e0b7d68b8","publicationDate":"2019-05-15T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20190025239.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20190025239/downloads/20190025239.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20190025239/downloads/20190025239.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20190025239/downloads/20190025239.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":135.32811},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":19890044272,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"d75d5676c63a40d59b13cead29df933b","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Man/System Technology And Life Support"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":19890044272,"ear":"NO","id":"b7ea3f66520b4ad49267bdc922095884","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"NSF ISI-85-21282","submissionId":19890044272,"id":"fa391f9a661e428ab959f684368f1d99","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NAS9-17326","submissionId":19890044272,"id":"8eb90683eca94c7396699f9f15e2fcaa","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"A differential approach to microcomputer test battery development and implementation","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-14T01:17:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":19890044272,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kennedy, R. S."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"6db9c69919ce48519365be768ab145e0"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":19890044272,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Baltzley, D. R."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"f1e53a458c354e749351fe27c2fab72d"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":19890044272,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Osteen, M. K."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"8ade43ebab9848969542d5d9790fef5b"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":19890044272,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Turnage, J. J."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"cf6a28a73095423db21c3285938fe09d"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":19890044272,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":"89A31643"},"created":"2013-08-14T01:17:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The present microcomputer-based performance test battery emphasizes psychometric theory and utility for repeated-measures applications during extended exposure to various environmental stressors. In the menu that has been defined at the current state of this system's development, there are more than 30 'qualified' mental tests which stabilize in less than 10 min and possess test-retest reliabilities greater than 0.7 for a three-minute test/work period. The battery encompasses tests of cognition, information processing, psychomotor skill, memory, mood, etc. Several of the tests have demonstrated sensitivity to chemoradiotherapy, sleep loss, hypoxia, amphetamines, thermal stress, sensory deprivation, altitude, fatigue, and alcohol use. Recommendations are presented for 6-, 12-, and 22-min batteries.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","meetings":[{"country":"United States","submissionId":19890044272,"endDate":"1988-10-28T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","name":"Annual Meeting of the Human Factors Society","location":"Anaheim, CA","id":"c75283e5f61d4d28bda41a65f9b44201","startDate":"1988-10-24T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":19890044272,"id":"dbd6de426dd147378bb18c77c3ebd153","publicationDate":"1988-01-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":134.43251},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"licenseType":"NO","submissionId":20205008023,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"8d26ed5303f447ff85b911091b2f84ea","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Behavioral Sciences"],"keywords":["cognition","normative data","fine motor skills"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20205008023,"ear":"NO","id":"c49bcf327d1c4bb0bcb25506741a778b","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2020-09-30T07:00:00.0000000+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"344494.02.01.10","submissionId":20205008023,"id":"0937c89d66b3438db6720eab820c9053","type":"WBS"}],"title":"The Cognition and Fine Motor Skills Test Batteries: Normative Data and Interdependencies","stiType":"TECHNICAL_MEMORANDUM","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2020-09-24T22:49:14.3007870+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"organizationId":"7f9c4cf9cb6f517db6b0fb40eb584c03","sequence":0,"submissionId":20205008023,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"","name":"Bettina L. Beard"},"organization":{"name":"Ames Research Center","location":"Mountain View, California, United States"}},"id":"d761468d76c94f71809a6fa90bb93427","userType":"CIVIL","userId":"cdd65d7d97064b9c8b4c62690f448793"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Technical Memorandum (TM)","technicalReviewType":"NASA_PEER_COMMITTEE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20205008023,"created":"2020-09-24T22:43:07.6705570+00:00","center":{"code":"ARC","name":"Ames Research Center","id":"4540cd94d24c4bf29f8773a27faf96b2"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Space mission success and safety relies upon astronaut functional state. Since spaceflight stressors affect cognitive processing and fine motor skills, NASA requires that measures of performance of these things remain within clinically accepted values (NASA STD 3001). NASA is in the process of developing two test batteries for the assessment of crew cognitive and fine motor skills before, during and after spaceflight. Toward that goal, the current project collected normative scores in 91 “astronaut-like” military and civilian pilots. \nThe Cognition Test Battery (CTB) contains ten sub-tests that measure a range of cognitive abilities. For five of the ten CTB sub-tests, we propose scores to improve the battery’s sensitivity. Among the ten sub-tests, response times were more highly correlated than accuracy scores. Principle component analysis of the correlations revealed that the first response time factor could explain over 40% of the total variance and appeared to represent the tendency of observers to try to respond more quickly. The first accuracy factor (explaining only 20%) gave a high weight to the higher level cognitive sub-tests and a negative weight to tasks associated with motor and lower level cognitive processing. \nThe Fine Motor Skills (FMS) test battery contains four sub-tests (Tracking, Pointing, Tracing, Rotating) performed on an Apple iPad tablet computer. Principle component analysis on the sub-test response time correlations revealed that the first two factors accounted for ~80% of the variance in performance. The first component captured overall speed on all four of the sub-tests. The second factor separated the sub-tasks into two groups (Drag-Point vs Trace-Rotate). Previous work found the first group response times correlated with that of a standard peg board task, while those of the other group did not. \nCorrelations were computed between the first FMS factor and the response time and accuracy scores from each CTB sub-task. Performing fine motor behaviors rapidly was significantly correlated with  the ability to perform many of the CTB sub-tests rapidly. This ability cannot be simple motor speed since scores on the Psychomotor Vigilance Test (PVT) subtask did not correlate with the ability to perform the other tasks rapidly. Speed on fine motor skills correlated significantly with accuracy on the short-term-memory sub-test. We hypothesize that eye movements, which can be regarded as a fine motor skill, may explain this relationship. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","publications":[{"submissionId":20205008023,"id":"5fdb91b3d00a4a6595750d2fe90a4b2f","publicationDate":"2019-06-01T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"NASA_TM20205008023.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20205008023/downloads/NASA_TM20205008023.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20205008023/downloads/NASA_TM20205008023.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20205008023/downloads/NASA_TM20205008023.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":96.18863},"keywords":[],"created":"2026-11-14T06:59:27.225510+00:00","distributionDate":"2026-11-13T06:59:27.225437+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"80NSSC18K0765","type":"funding"},{"number":"NNJ14ZSA001N","type":"funding"},{"number":"NASA NCC 9-58","type":"funding"},{"number":"80NSSC18K0765","type":"funding"},{"number":"NNJ14ZSA001N","type":"funding"},{"number":"NASA NCC 9-58","type":"funding"}],"distribution":"PUBLIC","stiType":"REPRINT","title":"Cognition Test Battery Survey: Development of a Single Alertness and Mood Score for Short- and Long-Duration Spaceflight","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Anish Pundyavana"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":1,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Yoni Gilad"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":2,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Alexander C. Stahn"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":3,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Mathias Basner"},"organization":{"name":""}}}],"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","stiTypeDetails":"Version of Record","modified":"2026-07-11T00:00:00","id":"77794093696409","sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app13042364"}],"publications":[{"volume":"13","pages":"2364","issue":"4","publisher":"MDPI AG","eissn":"2076-3417","publicationName":"Applied Sciences","publicationDate":"2023-12-02T00:00:00","doi":"10.3390/app13042364"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"chorus-2026-11-14-07-03"},{"_meta":{"score":81.32578},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":19880006969,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"56c90b9441644fa089cc9595d126e2fd","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Behavioral Sciences"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":19880006969,"ear":"NO","id":"4f5a45be6c1e4209ad9866bda8b6a5da","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-06-24T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["NASA-CR-172039","EOTR-86-4","NAS 1.26:172039","Report Number: NASA-CR-172039","Report Number: EOTR-86-4","Report Number: NAS 1.26:172039"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"NAS9-17326","submissionId":19880006969,"id":"6a705582b29d4da6824ba740e972df35","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"Stability, reliability and cross-mode correlations of tests in a recommended 8-minute performance assessment battery","stiType":"CONTRACTOR_REPORT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-09-05T17:57:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":19880006969,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Wilkes, R. L."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"d7011fe97cbd496da540299b3da0a5e4"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":19880006969,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kennedy, R. S."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"cc777a6478b34d31bd227d59cfc21968"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":19880006969,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Dunlap, W. P."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"6348338c10a74145be4fbe336386395e"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":19880006969,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Lane, N. E."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"eba59aef7b6b4cd2aaeb8618262e8d53"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Contractor Report (CR)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":19880006969,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":"88N16351"},"created":"2013-09-05T17:57:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"A need exists for an automated performance test system to study drugs, agents, treatments, and stresses of interest to the aviation, space, and environmental medical community. The purpose of this present study is to evaluate tests for inclusion in the NASA-sponsored Automated Performance Test System (APTS). Twenty-one subjects were tested over 10 replications with tests previously identified as good candidates for repeated-measure research. The tests were concurrently administered in paper-and-pencil and microcomputer modes. Performance scores for the two modes were compared. Data from trials 1 to 10 were examined for indications of test stability and reliability. Nine of the ten APT system tests achieved stability. Reliabilities were generally high. Cross-correlation of microbased tests with traditional paper-and-pencil versions revealed similarity of content within tests in the different modes, and implied at least three cognition and two motor factors. This protable, inexpensive, rugged, computerized battery of tests is recommended for use in repeated-measures studies of environmental and drug effects on performance. Identification of other tests compatible with microcomputer testing and potentially capable of tapping previously unidentified factors is recommended. Documentation of APTS sensitivity to environmental agents is available for more than a dozen facilities and is reported briefly. Continuation of such validation remains critical in establishing the efficacy of APTS tests.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","publications":[{"submissionId":19880006969,"id":"9f38480b281c4fec98cdf0f6a68aff72","publicationDate":"1986-04-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"19880006969.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/19880006969/downloads/19880006969.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/19880006969/downloads/19880006969.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/19880006969/downloads/19880006969.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":77.98681},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":19880007070,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"8c4520f4c6224c28a260649b4cba136f","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Computer Programming And Software"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":19880007070,"ear":"NO","id":"ac60e304049748bbbb466c40e6d127e8","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-06-24T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["NASA-CR-172040","NAS 1.26:172040","Report Number: NASA-CR-172040","Report Number: NAS 1.26:172040"],"fundingNumbers":[{"number":"NAS9-17326","submissionId":19880007070,"id":"aaa8d392f7b34a10a3659480d10c0eca","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"Development of a menu of performance tests self-administered on a portable microcomputer","stiType":"CONTRACTOR_REPORT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-09-05T17:58:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":19880007070,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Wilkes, Robert L."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"eedbb5b5ae364cf1b0644d303b7feaa4"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":19880007070,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kuntz, Lois-Ann"},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"2cf82bef45c04097b3a74cefc216f0b5"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":19880007070,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kennedy, Robert S."},"organization":{"name":"Essex Corp.","location":"Orlando, FL, United States"}},"id":"ddd753cc6e194cfd9249481ede0fc0c3"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Contractor Report (CR)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":19880007070,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":"88N16452"},"created":"2013-09-05T17:58:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Eighteen cognitive, motor, and information processing performance subtests were screened for self-administration over 10 trials by 16 subjects. When altered presentation forms of the same test were collectively considered, the battery composition was reduced to 10 distinctly different measures. A fully automated microbased testing system was employed in presenting the battery of subtests. Successful self-administration of the battery provided for the field testing of the automated system and facilitated convenient data collection. Total test administration time was 47.2 minutes for each session. Results indicated that nine of the tests stabilized, but for a short battery of tests only five are recommended for use in repeated-measures research. The five recommended tests include: the Tapping series, Number Comparison, Short-term Memory, Grammatical Reasoning, and 4-Choice Reaction Time. These tests can be expected to reveal three factors: (1) cognition, (2) processing quickness, and (3) motor. All the tests stabilized in 24 minutes, or approximately two 12-minute sessions.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","publications":[{"submissionId":19880007070,"id":"fc2c84475d3949198294b0bfd1c830d9","publicationDate":"1987-10-16T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"19880007070.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/19880007070/downloads/19880007070.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/19880007070/downloads/19880007070.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/19880007070/downloads/19880007070.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"}],"aggregations":{"created":{"buckets":[{"key_as_str